#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# min_limit

#% text
# `min_limit(f, x -> a)` gives the limit inferior of `f` as `x` tends to `a`: the smallest value that `f` keeps coming back to near `a`.

#% text
# `min_limit(f, x -> a, direction: from_above)` and `direction: from_below` take one side.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# When the limit exists, `min_limit` gives it.

#% item
# A sum of a function with a limit and a bounded oscillation `c sin(u)`, `c cos(u)` with `u` tending to an infinity gives the limit minus the amplitude.

#% item
# At a finite point without a direction, the smaller of the two one-sided values is given.

#% item
# When the value cannot be decided, the call stays unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# An oscillation that settles between -1 and 1:

#% cell
min_limit(sin(x) - 1/x, x -> inf)
#% output
#= -1

#% text
# A pole of odd order:

#% cell
min_limit(1/x, x -> 0)
#% output
#= -inf

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The amplitude of a combination of sine and cosine:

#% cell
min_limit(3*sin(x) + 4*cos(x), x -> inf)
#% output
#= -5

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `min_limit(f)` is `-max_limit(-f)`:

#% cell
[min_limit(cos(2*x)/2, x -> inf), -max_limit(-cos(2*x)/2, x -> inf)]
#% output
#= [-1/2, -1/2]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `max_limit`, `limit`
